ok: association -- a table of dumped constants is text derived from generated code
ok: association -- a census of the decisions a kernel was built from moves with them
ok: association -- a golden of nothing but claims is the test's verdict, not its output
ok: association -- blank lines do not make a verdict golden into output
ok: association -- an empty golden is not output either
ok: stem -- a plain source
ok: stem -- a select real
ok: stem -- a select stub
ok: golden -- a C kernel is a member by its markers, under any name
ok: golden -- a CUDA kernel names the launch vocabulary
ok: golden -- a Metal kernel names its address spaces
ok: golden -- a low-level IR dump is a member by its loop headers and assignments
ok: golden -- a routine log carries both the IR line and the C rendering
ok: golden -- a snapshot is a member by its extension whatever it holds
ok: golden -- a declaring extension names the substrate; markers stay as evidence
ok: golden -- HIP spells CUDA's launch vocabulary, and is still HIP
ok: golden -- a .hip snapshot is its own family, not CUDA's
ok: golden -- an .ll snapshot is declared even when the dump is empty
ok: golden -- a verdict quoting the kernel's vocabulary is prose, not Metal
ok: golden -- a claim about how a constant is spelled is not a constant
ok: golden -- a PASS column is a verdict however it reads
ok: golden -- a memory-mode table is not device code
ok: golden -- a numeric table of shapes is not a loop nest
ok: golden -- a failure line is a verdict too
ok: source -- assert_emits pins its contains argument
ok: source -- assert_omits pins the fragment that must be absent
ok: source -- the has idiom: a predicate closing over the source
ok: source -- a predicate taking the source as a parameter pins at a tainted call site
ok: source -- the same predicate over text that is not generated source pins nothing
ok: source -- a substring test straight against the read is a pin
ok: source -- a sprintf format is a pinned fragment with a hole in it
ok: source -- text the scan cannot name marks the itemisation partial, without losing the file
ok: source -- taint reaches through a helper that returns the source
ok: source -- a routine name is not a pinned fragment
ok: source -- a test opening build_files/ itself is a member, and is told apart
ok: source -- counting occurrences with ~pattern pins the fragment counted
ok: source -- a concatenation with a literal part is a fragment with a hole in it
ok: source -- the haystack may reach the test through a local binding
ok: source -- a predicate over the backend's NAME pins nothing, however it is spelled
ok: source -- a source reached through a tuple pattern is still a source
ok: source -- a test binding its own build_file is not reading the artifact directory
ok: source -- the qualified Utils.build_file is a read of the artifact directory
ok: source -- a module alias of the reader is the reader
ok: source -- an alias of an alias is an alias
ok: source -- an alias bound in expression position counts too
ok: source -- an alias of Utils is a direct artifact read
ok: source -- an unqualified read is not the reader, which is what the qualifier is for
ok: source -- rendering the emitter's document is reaching generated text
ok: source -- a dump printer is an emitter too, under whatever alias
ok: source -- a test emitting to a golden pins nothing and is still a member
ok: source -- an unqualified to_doc is the test's own, not an emitter
ok: source -- an inline emitter render in the haystack still pins its fragment
ok: source -- an inline build_files read in the haystack still pins its fragment
ok: source -- a helper that hard-codes the fragment pins it, taking the source as its parameter
ok: source -- a helper that slices on a banner and tests its own parameter pins both
ok: source -- a fragment named through a binding is still that fragment
ok: source -- a test that reads no generated source is not a member
ok: source -- naming the reader in a comment is not reading it
